{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.55,
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 28,
          "low": 8,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 30,
          "low": 4,
          "param": "n_iter"
        }
      ],
      "why": "Fully pipelined gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput = Fmax. To hit >=1 MSPS we need Fmax >= 1 MHz, which is trivially met, but area (luts+ffs) is the primary objective. A fully pipelined CORDIC has N+2 pipeline registers, so FF count scales linearly with n_iter and data_width. The accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-10) is the binding constraint that sets the minimum n_iter and data_width. I want to explore the full range to find the minimum-area point that satisfies accuracy, and also map the trade-off curve where higher accuracy (more n_iter or wider data) costs more area. The angle_guard and frac_guard affect both accuracy and area (wider angle path = more LUTs for atan table, more FFs in pipeline). Rounding mode affects accuracy slightly. This is the core family for this spec since it directly trades area vs accuracy at full throughput."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 28,
          "low": 8,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 30,
          "low": 4,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 8,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, so it has ceil(N/m)+2 pipeline registers instead of N+2. This can significantly reduce FF count (and thus luts_plus_ffs) compared to fully pipelined, at the cost of higher latency (ceil(N/m)+2 vs N+2 cycles). Since the spec only requires >=1 MSPS throughput (not low latency), and area is the primary objective, pipelined_m with larger m could yield much smaller area while still meeting throughput (1 result/cycle). The trade-off is that larger m means fewer pipeline registers but potentially tighter timing (longer combinational path between registers), which could lower Fmax. However, since we only need >=1 MSPS, even a modest Fmax reduction is acceptable. This family should explore the area-accuracy trade-off at reduced FF cost. The m parameter is the key differentiator: m=2,3,4,5,6,7,8 give progressively fewer registers. I want to see where the area savings plateau and whether Fmax drops below 1 MHz for large m."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires >=1 MSPS throughput and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area (luts+ffs) as the primary objective. Both pipelined and pipelined_m produce 1 result/cycle, so throughput = Fmax. The key insight is that pipelined_m can reduce FF count by registering every m stages instead of every stage, directly reducing area. Since latency is not a constraint, larger m values are attractive for area reduction. The accuracy constraint (2^-10) sets a floor on n_iter and data_width. I'm allocating 55% to pipelined (the baseline, fully pipelined) and 45% to pipelined_m (the area-optimized variant). Both families share the same accuracy-determining parameters (data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding), so the accuracy constraint will be similar, but the area (especially FFs) will differ significantly. The full ranges are used because I have no prior information and need to discover where the accuracy constraint binds and where the area trade-off curve lies. The hypervolume objective rewards covering the trade-off curve, so I want to explore both the minimum-area feasible point and the higher-accuracy/higher-area points."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The feasible Pareto front is well-established and covers the required trade-off space. The minimum area design (pipelined_m, luts_plus_ffs=874) meets the throughput constraint (68.5 MSPS >= 1) and the accuracy constraint (10.7 bits > 10 bits required for 2^-10 error). The front extends to high accuracy (23.3 bits) with higher area, providing a complete map of the area-accuracy trade-off. Since this is the final round, no further exploration is possible or necessary."
,
  "next_plan": null
}
